</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The F1-score threshold plays a crucial role in ensuring the quality of training data.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Setting the threshold too low introduces excessive low-quality data, which can hinder model performance. Conversely, setting the threshold too high risks discarding a substantial amount of otherwise useful data, thereby reducing the diversity of the training set and negatively impacting model training. This is because traditional OCR models are not flawless (unable to recognize formulas) and some predictions from our model may only omit minor elements, such as page numbers or headers, while still containing all of the main content. To investigate this, we conducted a comprehensive ablation study on different F1-score thresholds, as shown in <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ The F1-score threshold plays a crucial role in ensuring the quality of training data. ‣ 3.2.2 The Iterative Self-improvement Stage ‣ 3.2 Ablation Studies and Observations ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ POINTS-Reader: Distillation-Free Adaptation of Vision-Language Models for Document Conversion" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>. The results indicate that both overly low and overly high thresholds are detrimental to model performance.</p>
</div>
